  "Question": "Angie, Bridget, Carlos, and Diego are seated at random around a square table, one person to a side. What is the probability that Angie and Carlos are seated opposite each other?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Only Carlos's position relative to Angie matters. Wherever Angie sits, Carlos is equally likely to occupy any of the three remaining seats. These are the seat to her left, the seat to her right, and the seat opposite her. Exactly one of them is opposite her, so the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\]\nThe answer is choice B.",
